### Ambiguous payloads — the proxy should NOT block these outright (expect 2xx/3xx),
### but each should score as "suspicious" and raise the client's reputation.
#
# These sit between attacks.http (clearly malicious -> blocked) and benign.http
# (clearly fine -> free). They carry a SQL keyword or a comment marker but no
# full injection, so the simple rules in detector.py rate them SUSPICIOUS: the
# request is forwarded, but the score goes up. Fire enough of them from one IP
# and the reputation throttle kicks in — tarpit first, then 429. Watch the
# proxy log for "suspicious", "THROTTLED" and finally "BANNED".

@proxy = http://127.0.0.1:8080

### A lone SQL keyword in a search box (could be a real search, could be probing)
GET {{proxy}}/search?q=select

### Semicolon + a read keyword: looks like a stacked query, but harmless on its own
GET {{proxy}}/items?id=1; select 1

### SQL comment sequence (--) buried in otherwise normal text
GET {{proxy}}/search?q=buy-one--get-one

### Double dash inside a form field
POST {{proxy}}/register
Content-Type: application/x-www-form-urlencoded

name=cool--dude&email=cool@example.com&password=pw123456

### Block comment /* */ in a free-text note
POST {{proxy}}/api/order
Content-Type: application/json

{"note": "table 5 /* VIP guest */"}

### UNION without SELECT — incomplete, so not an obvious attack
GET {{proxy}}/items?cols=id,name union all

### A bare SQL keyword in a header
GET {{proxy}}/
User-Agent: scanner/1.0 drop

### Database-metadata reference, no working injection (example_metadata detector)
GET {{proxy}}/search?q=information_schema
